docs: labels, names, and what belongs in DNS

A shaping note from the namespace conversation. Doc-only.

The criterion the rest of it hangs on: **a label belongs in DNS if and only if
it needs its own origin; otherwise it is a path.** A hostname buys exactly one
thing a path does not — an origin, with its own storage partition, passkey scope
and same-origin boundary. Everything else it appears to buy (hierarchy,
legibility, grouping) a path gives for free and without a certificate row.

That resolves the Atlas question: library labels need origins (per-label storage,
a passkey scoped to one editable data-bottle rather than all of them), so they
are hostnames. Atlas category views do not — a category board is a `/`-separated
label list unioned into a filter over a shared index, read-only, no per-view
storage or credential — so they are paths. Minting sub-sub-domains to look deeper
at a town is path information wearing a hostname, and unwinds back to a folder
having cost a SAN row on the way.

Also in the note:

- **Labels are not names.** Three things holding `north` is a set, not a
  collision. A label is many-to-many and pointless to reserve; a name is one
  holder, established by signature. So an address is a rendezvous coordinate, not
  a claim — which is what `composer/bottle-book.mjs` already says — and the
  squatting race dissolves, because linkage between two `north`s is signed
  content rather than a fact in DNS. It also unifies an Atlas category path and a
  library label: same structure, differing only in whether storage is involved.

- **The apex keeps its bottle space deliberately empty**, so the label space is
  the visitor's and there is nothing to arbitrate. Canonical bottles stay
  distributable — a bottle travels by its bytes, not its address.

- **`library` is a name, not a level.** If a node can publish `library1` and
  `library2` there is no plain `library`. Keeping it out of the hostname halves
  the SAN row count, against a pack capped at 50 that aborts rather than
  truncates.

- **A matrix, and DNS is a tree.** moniker × facet can only be expressed in one
  ordering. Facet-as-parent costs rows = cities × facets (closed set);
  moniker-as-parent costs cities × monikers (open-ended). The current orientation
  is already the cheap one, so the tempting inversion is the expensive direction.

- **Wildcard fall-through** works the way it was hoped: DNS consults a wildcard
  only with no exact match and no closer enclosing node (RFC 1034), so served
  names win automatically. The limit is that a wildcard covers exactly one label,
  so an apex catch-all is a floor for one level, not a contraction of the deeper
  scheme.

- **Nodes publish their own configuration** — wings live on non-main branches, so
  a small signed manifest (branch → wing name, readable via `git-enough`) answers
  both "what are your wings" and "what rows would you cost", the latter being
  what the row budget needs before onboarding rather than after.

Open items are kept open, including the one that blocks all of it: neither GitHub
Pages nor Cloudflare Pages will take a wildcard custom domain, so an unbounded
label space needs a Worker, and that decision is independent of the naming.

